\DOC induction_for_type

\TYPE {induction_for_type : hol_type -> thm -> thm}

\SYNOPSIS
The induction theorem of one type of a family of them.

\DESCRIBE
The induction theorem of a family of datatypes that were defined
together is about all of them at once, with a predicate for each, which
{MATCH_MP_TAC} cannot aim at a goal about one of them. A call
{induction_for_type ty ith} takes the predicates of the other types to be
trivially true and simplifies, which leaves the theorem for {ty} alone,
with the premises that were only about the other types gone. For a
family of one type it is the theorem itself.

What that costs is the hypotheses about the other types: induction on an
{exp} whose {Call} holds an {elist} gives no hypothesis about the list,
because the predicate on lists was taken to be true. That is the weak
scheme, and it is what {induction} on one of a family gives in Rocq as
well; {INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC} is the strong one.

\FAILURE
Fails if no conclusion of {ith} is about the type {ty}.

\EXAMPLE
With {exp} and {elist} defined together:
{
  # induction_of `:exp`;;
  val it : thm =
    |- forall P0 P1.
           (forall a. P0 (Num a)) /\
           (forall a. P1 a ==> P0 (Call a)) /\
           P1 Nil /\
           (forall a0 a1. P0 a0 /\ P1 a1 ==> P1 (Cons a0 a1))
           ==> (forall x0. P0 x0) /\ (forall x1. P1 x1)

  # induction_for_type `:exp` (induction_of `:exp`);;
  val it : thm =
    |- forall P0.
           (forall a. P0 (Num a)) /\ (forall a. P0 (Call a))
           ==> (forall x0. P0 x0)
}

\SEEALSO
INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC, induction_of, INDUCT_ON_TAC.

\ENDDOC
